---
name: report
group: general
description: where a report, a list, a table, a draft or an answer with citations goes, how to edit it, and how to show it
tools: write_file
---
Name a file after its content, such as `report.md`, `timeline.md` or `sources.md`. Write
Markdown for formatted text. A list or a table can be its own file.

{{ tool('patch_file') }} takes name, old_string, new_string and replace_all. It takes no
expected_version. Copy old_string from the text of the file, without the `N|` prefix that
{{ tool('read_file') }} puts before each line. When a patch fails because old_string does
not match or matches more than once, read that part of the file again and send a corrected
patch. Do not write the whole file again because a patch failed.

For each listed item, verify every constraint against the source text before you include it.
Merge reports about the same item.
Verify in the source each property that the request requires of an item.
A related property does not establish the required one.
Count one case, event or entity once, even when several sources report its stages.
List the identifiers of its stages together, such as court names and file numbers.
Give fewer items when the sources establish fewer matches, and state the shortage.
Keep a related item that fails a constraint outside the matching list, and name its failed constraint.
For each claim, verify its subject, action, location, and date in the same source passage.
Do not assign a statement about one subject to another.

`todo-search-list.md` · `report.md`

Complete one small group of senders before you start the next group. Search, save the candidates, verify and cite the sources, then patch the report.

For a sender roster, save each candidate's sender, collection, file hash, and next action before verification. Record the search scope for an absence and the error for a failed read. After citation succeeds, patch the report and mark the checklist entry complete.

After a summary of earlier steps, the list of saved files after the newest message gives
the name and the version of each file. Read the report and the checklist with
{{ tool('read_file') }} before you continue. Do not search again for an item that the
checklist marks done.

Example. The person asks for one file for each of five cases. Write `case-1.md` to
`case-5.md`, one {{ tool('write_file') }} call for each file. Then call
{{ tool('finish_with_report') }} with `case-1.md` and its version, the other four files
with their versions in additional, and one sentence in message. The answer lists the five
files, so do not copy their text into the chat. When the person later asks to correct
`case-2.md`, patch only that file. The other files keep their versions.

When a {{ tool('write_file') }} call that creates a file returns success false, the file
does not exist. Read the error, correct the content, and call {{ tool('write_file') }}
again without expected_version. Do not call {{ tool('patch_file') }} before a write of that
file succeeds.
```json
{"success":false,"name":"cases.md","error":"The file was not changed, because a citation handle does not resolve."}
```
```json
{"name":"cases.md","content":"The court dismissed the claim [D2]."}
```
